# Target C-II — outer-pocket degree moment forced by fixed-pair FREE reuse

**Date:** 2026-09-18
**Status:** proof-only research candidate; theorem-state authority unchanged
**Scope:** canonical post-ZF outer-pocket decomposition, with application to the proof-only fixed-pair `PAIR-REUSE` ancestry population
**Dependencies:** canonical Section 67 outer-pocket/Oler decomposition; elementary reflection plus planar isoperimetry; `target_c_halfplus_outer_charge_full_activation_2026-09-18.md` for the strongest application
**Certificate:** `research/verify_target_c_tooth_reflection_halfplus_pocket_degree_moment_2026_09_18.py`
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.`

The current fixed-pair theorem charges more than `D` distinct fully inactive
outer-face ancestry vertices to

\[
Z_{\rm ext}+{2\over\sqrt3}H_{\rm out}-{b-P_F\over2}.
\]

That still leaves the internal outer-pocket area as a possible linear-scale sink.  The
present note charges the **population itself** to a sharper discrete OP currency: the
second moment of the outer-pocket degree excesses.

For an OP pocket `P`, write

\[
k_P\ge3,
\qquad
r_P:=k_P-3,
\qquad
\ell_P:=k_P-1=r_P+2,
\]

where `ell_P` is the number of old unit-contact edges on the pocket chain.  Put

\[
d_P:=\sum_P r_P,
\qquad
S_2:=\sum_P r_P^2.
\]

Then every triangular pocket (`r_P=0`) contains **no** assigned contact-isolated
source point, and in general

\[
\boxed{
\pi\sqrt3\,Z_{\rm in}
\le
S_2+8d_P.
}
\tag{PD.1}
\]

A sharper scalar consequence is

\[
\boxed{
Z_{\rm in}
\le {1\over\pi\sqrt3}
\max\{9d_P,(d_P+2)^2\}
\qquad(d_P\ge1),
}
\tag{PD.2}
\]

with `Z_in=0` when `d_P=0`.

Applied to the current truncation-free fixed-pair ancestry theorem, for fixed
`K>h_*` one gets

\[
\boxed{
S_2+8d_P
\ge
\pi\sqrt3\left(\nu_KD+{U_1+U_2\over2}-Z_{\rm ext}-o(D)\right),
\qquad
\nu_K:=1+{\delta_*\over4}\left(1-{h_*\over K}\right).
}
\tag{PD.3}
\]

Hence if `Z_ext/D<=zeta+o(1)` with `zeta<nu_K`,

\[
\boxed{
{d_P\over\sqrt D}
\ge
\sqrt{\pi\sqrt3\,(\nu_K-\zeta)}-o(1).
}
\tag{PD.4}
\]

Thus the fixed-pair heavy branch cannot place its linear outer-face ancestry
population inside the force hull while keeping both external population and
nontriangular pocket complexity below square-root scale.

---

## 1. One pocket: isolate capacity depends only on its contact-chain length

Use the canonical Section-67 hull augmentation.  One convex outer pocket `P` has
boundary

\[
[u,v]\cup (u=p_0,p_1,\ldots,p_{\ell}=v),
\]

where `[u,v]` is the added force-hull side and the complementary chain has `ell`
unit-contact edges.  Write

\[
s:=|u-v|,
\qquad
A_P:=\operatorname{area}(P),
\qquad
z_P:=\#\{\text{assigned contact-isolated source points in }P\}.
\]

Since the contact chain joins the chord endpoints,

\[
s\le\ell.
\tag{1.1}
\]

Canonical Section 67.6 applies Oler to the pocket with its boundary source vertices
and assigned isolates and gives

\[
z_P
\le {A_P\over a_0}+{s+\ell\over2}+1-k_P,
\qquad
a_0={\sqrt3\over2}.
\tag{1.2}
\]

Because `k_P=ell+1`, this is

\[
z_P
\le {A_P\over a_0}+{s-\ell\over2}
\le {A_P\over a_0}.
\tag{1.3}
\]

There is a second elementary estimate that removes the chord from the perimeter.
Reflect `P` across the line through `[u,v]`.  For a nondegenerate pocket the two
contact chains, one on each side of the chord, form the boundary of the doubled
domain; if a chain has a collinear chord-touch, split at the touch points and apply
the same estimate componentwise.  The total doubled area is `2A_P` and the total
non-chord boundary length is `2ell`.  Planar isoperimetry, followed by
`sum L_i^2 <= (sum L_i)^2` in the split case, therefore gives

\[
2A_P\le{(2\ell)^2\over4\pi},
\qquad
\boxed{A_P\le{\ell^2\over2\pi}.}
\tag{1.4}
\]

Combining (1.3) and (1.4),

\[
\boxed{
z_P\le{\ell^2\over\pi\sqrt3}
={ (r_P+2)^2\over\pi\sqrt3}.}
\tag{1.5}
\]

For a triangular outer pocket `r_P=0`, the right side is

\[
{4\over\pi\sqrt3}<1.
\]

Since `z_P` is an integer,

\[
\boxed{r_P=0\quad\Longrightarrow\quad z_P=0.}
\tag{1.6}
\]

This is the key removal of the otherwise linear baseline contributed by a large
family of triangular pockets.

---

## 2. Global second-moment payment

Let `P_+` be the nontriangular outer pockets, and let

\[
t:=|P_+|.
\]

Every `r_P` on `P_+` is a positive integer, so

\[
t\le d_P.
\tag{2.1}
\]

Triangular pockets contribute no isolates by (1.6).  Sum (1.5) over `P_+`:

\[
\begin{aligned}
\pi\sqrt3\,Z_{\rm in}
&\le\sum_{P\in P_+}(r_P+2)^2\\
&=S_2+4d_P+4t\\
&\le S_2+8d_P.
\end{aligned}
\tag{2.2}
\]

This proves (PD.1).

Because the `r_P` are nonnegative,

\[
S_2\le d_P^2,
\tag{2.3}
\]

which already gives the convenient finite bound

\[
Z_{\rm in}\le {d_P^2+8d_P\over\pi\sqrt3}.
\tag{2.4}
\]

The exact scalar maximum is slightly better.  For a positive integer composition
`d=r_1+...+r_t`, the quantity to maximize is

\[
\sum_i(r_i+2)^2.
\]

Merging two parts changes this sum by

\[
(r+s+2)^2-(r+2)^2-(s+2)^2=2rs-4.
\tag{2.5}
\]

If some part is at least two and there is more than one part, merge that part with
any other part; then `rs>=2`, so (2.5) is nonnegative.  Repeating this operation
reduces to the single part `d` without decreasing the objective.  If no part is at
least two, all parts equal one.  Hence a maximizer is attained at one of these two
endpoints:

\[
\boxed{
\max_{r_i\ge1,\ \sum r_i=d}
\sum_i(r_i+2)^2
=\max\{(d+2)^2,9d\}.
}
\tag{2.6}
\]

This proves (PD.2).  In particular, for `d_P>=4`,

\[
\boxed{
Z_{\rm in}\le{(d_P+2)^2\over\pi\sqrt3}.}
\tag{2.7}
\]

No pocket-area variable remains in (2.2) or (2.7).

---

## 3. Insert the truncation-free `PAIR-REUSE` outer population

The current fixed-pair outer-charge theorem is stronger than the earlier matching-only
population count.  For fixed `K>h_*`, put

\[
\alpha_K=1-{h_*\over K},
\qquad
\nu_K=1+{\delta_*\alpha_K\over4}.
\tag{3.1}
\]

Let `U_1,U_2` be the upper-gate counts of the two extreme FREE ancestries.  The
truncation-free ancestry theorem gives one ancestry containing at least

\[
\nu_KD+{U_1+U_2\over2}-o(D)
\tag{3.2}
\]

distinct old fully inactive outer-face source points.  At most `Z_ext` of these lie
outside the force hull.  Consequently

\[
\boxed{
Z_{\rm in}
\ge
\nu_KD+{U_1+U_2\over2}-Z_{\rm ext}-o(D).
}
\tag{3.3}
\]

Insert (3.3) into (PD.1):

\[
\boxed{
S_2+8d_P
\ge
\pi\sqrt3\left(
\nu_KD+{U_1+U_2\over2}-Z_{\rm ext}-o(D)
\right).
}
\tag{PD.3}
\]

This is strictly stronger at every finite second cutoff than spending only the
near-unit parity matching.  The latter remains a valid finite substatement: if `M` is
the half-plus matching, then

\[
S_2+8d_P\ge\pi\sqrt3\,(2|M|-Z_{\rm ext}).
\tag{3.4}
\]

For the scalar consequence, suppose

\[
{Z_{\rm ext}\over D}\le\zeta+o(1),
\qquad
0\le\zeta<\nu_K.
\]

Then (PD.2) and (3.3) force

\[
\boxed{
{d_P\over\sqrt D}
\ge
\sqrt{\pi\sqrt3\,(\nu_K-\zeta)}-o(1).
}
\tag{PD.4}
\]

The nonnegative upper-gate term in (PD.3) can only strengthen this floor.

For the explicit fixed choice `K=400`,

\[
\nu_{400}=1.001042380190805\ldots,
\]

so `Z_ext=o(D)` forces

\[
\boxed{
d_P\ge(2.333895905623207\ldots-o(1))\sqrt D.
}
\tag{3.5}
\]

Taking the fixed-cutoff envelope `K->infinity` after `D->infinity`,

\[
\nu_\infty
=1+{\delta_*\over4}
=1.001043253258118\ldots,
\]

and the coefficient becomes

\[
\boxed{
\sqrt{\pi\sqrt3\,\nu_\infty}
=2.333896923386203\ldots .
}
\tag{3.6}
\]

Thus, on the fixed-pair heavy branch,

\[
Z_{\rm ext}=o(D)
\quad\Longrightarrow\quad
x_B-a=d_P=\Omega(\sqrt D).
\tag{3.7}
\]

The exact PC/HB remainder also reads

\[
e_{BB}-Q-2(d_H+s_Z)=d_P+c_B,
\]

so the same square-root pocket term is visible in the loaded-hull slack rather than
only in an area variable.

---

## 4. The stronger second-moment endpoint

Equation (PD.3) retains more information than the scalar square-root floor.  If

\[
Z_{\rm ext}=o(D),
\qquad
d_P=o(D),
\]

then for fixed `K`

\[
\boxed{
S_2
\ge
(\pi\sqrt3\,\nu_K-o(1))D.
}
\tag{4.1}
\]

At the fixed-cutoff envelope the leading coefficient is

\[
\boxed{
\pi\sqrt3\,\nu_\infty
=5.447074848991583\ldots .
}
\tag{4.2}
\]

Let

\[
r_{\max}:=\max_P r_P.
\]

Since

\[
S_2\le r_{\max}d_P,
\tag{4.3}
\]

any regime with `d_P<=C sqrt(D)` and `Z_ext=o(D)` has one nontriangular outer pocket
with

\[
\boxed{
{r_{\max}\over\sqrt D}
\ge
{\pi\sqrt3\,\nu_K\over C}-o(1).
}
\tag{4.4}
\]

So the critical square-root regime necessarily concentrates pocket degree into a
mesoscopic carrier; it cannot distribute the half-plus endpoint population over only
bounded-complexity pockets.

---

## 5. Equality-scale localization

The preceding inequalities identify the scalar endpoint sharply.  Fix `K` and
suppose along a `PAIR-REUSE` sequence that

\[
Z_{\rm ext}=o(D),
\qquad
{d_P\over\sqrt D}
\longrightarrow
c_K:=\sqrt{\pi\sqrt3\,\nu_K}.
\tag{5.1}
\]

Then every inequality used above is asymptotically tight at leading order:

\[
{Z_{\rm in}\over D}\to\nu_K,
\qquad
{U_1+U_2\over D}\to0,
\qquad
{S_2\over d_P^2}\to1,
\qquad
{r_{\max}\over d_P}\to1.
\tag{5.2}
\]

Indeed, (3.3) gives the lower population floor, while (2.7) and (5.1) give the
scalar upper floor.  These bounds also force `(U_1+U_2)/D->0`, and
(2.2)--(2.3) then squeeze `S_2/d_P^2` to one.  Finally
`S_2<=r_max d_P<=d_P^2` gives the last limit.

Choose a pocket `P_*` with `r_{P_*}=r_max`.  The remaining degree excess is `o(sqrt D)`.
Applying (1.5) and (2.2) to the remaining nontriangular pockets shows that they contain
only `o(D)` assigned isolates.  Hence

\[
\boxed{
{z_{P_*}\over D}\to\nu_K.
}
\tag{5.3}
\]

Moreover `ell_*=r_{P_*}+2=(c_K+o(1))sqrt(D)`.  Oler gives
`z_{P_*}<=A_*/a_0`, while reflected isoperimetry gives
`A_*<=ell_*^2/(2pi)`.  The constants match exactly because

\[
{c_K^2\over2\pi}
={\sqrt3\over2}\nu_K
=a_0\nu_K.
\]

Therefore

\[
\boxed{
{A_*\over D}\to a_0\nu_K,
\qquad
{2\pi A_*\over\ell_*^2}\to1,
\qquad
{a_0z_{P_*}\over A_*}\to1.
}
\tag{5.4}
\]

Thus the minimal square-root `d_P` endpoint is rigid at the scalar level: one
`sqrt(D)`-chain outer pocket carries essentially all of the linear internal inactive
population, saturates reflected isoperimetry, and saturates triangular-lattice area
capacity.  The chord ratio is also forced at this endpoint.  Let `s_*` be the force-hull chord
length and choose the unique `theta_* in (0,pi)` satisfying

\[
{s_*\over\ell_*}={\sin\theta_*\over\theta_*}.
\tag{5.5}
\]

The fixed-length/fixed-chord Dido estimate follows by completing the pocket chain with
the complementary circular arc and applying planar isoperimetry:

\[
{A_*\over\ell_*^2}
\le f(\theta_*),
\qquad
f(\theta):={\theta-\sin\theta\cos\theta\over4\theta^2}.
\tag{5.6}
\]

A direct derivative gives

\[
f'(\theta)=
{\cos\theta(\sin\theta-\theta\cos\theta)\over2\theta^3}.
\tag{5.7}
\]

Since `sin(theta)-theta cos(theta)>0` on `(0,pi)`, `f` has its unique maximum at
`theta=pi/2`, where `f(pi/2)=1/(2pi)`.  Equation (5.4) therefore forces

\[
\boxed{
\theta_*\to{\pi\over2},
\qquad
{s_*\over\ell_*}\to{2\over\pi},
\qquad
{\ell_*-s_*\over\ell_*}\to1-{2\over\pi}.
}
\tag{5.8}
\]

Thus the critical pocket also creates an actual outer-walk chain--chord detour.  Since
canonical Section 63 decomposes the total outer-walk detour as the sum of the
consecutive hull-interval deficits, this one pocket contributes one of its
nonnegative summands.  Hence

\[
\boxed{
b-P_F
\ge \ell_*-s_*
=\left(\left(1-{2\over\pi}\right)c_K+o(1)\right)\sqrt D.
}
\tag{5.9}
\]

At the fixed-cutoff envelope this coefficient is

\[
\boxed{
\left(1-{2\over\pi}\right)
\sqrt{\pi\sqrt3\,\nu_\infty}
=0.848091995290680\ldots .
}
\tag{5.10}
\]

No Hausdorff convergence of the pocket boundary is claimed; (5.4) and (5.8) are the
proved scalar saturation statements.  They are enough to show that the minimum-degree
endpoint cannot hide in nearly straight pockets.

This is a much narrower regression target than an arbitrary linear-area outer-pocket
sink.

---

## 6. What remains

The new bridge does not close C-II.  Its useful alternatives are now:

1. `Z_ext` itself pays a linear fraction of the half-plus endpoint population;
2. `d_P` is supercritical compared with `sqrt(D)`, producing explicit nontriangular
   outer-walk complexity; or
3. at the critical `sqrt(D)` scale, one mesoscopic outer pocket carries a linear
   isolate population, saturates reflected isoperimetry and Oler density at leading
   order, has chord/chain ratio `2/pi+o(1)`, and contributes
   `(0.84809...-o(1))sqrt(D)` actual chain--chord detour at the envelope.

The third endpoint is the sharp next geometric target.  A closure would need to rule
out that scalar-saturated pocket using the fact that its boundary chain consists of
**actual unit contacts tied to the force core**, or use it to build a protected
same-cardinality strict sub-`D` replacement.  Density or isoperimetry alone cannot do
that, because near-triangular interior packings can asymptotically saturate both.

**Disposition:** proved finite outer-pocket population/degree-moment bridge and its
half-plus reflection consequences; theorem-state authority unchanged.  C-II and Level
I remain open.
